# 20. Valid Parentheses

- **LeetCode:** https://leetcode.com/problems/valid-parentheses/
- **Topic:** Stack, Strings
- **Language:** Java
- **Status:** Solved

## 1. My Intuition

We need to check whether the given brackets are properly opened and closed.

My idea was to use a Stack.

Whenever I encounter an opening bracket, I push it into the stack.

Whenever I encounter a closing bracket, I compare it with the most recently opened bracket.

If they match, remove the opening bracket from the stack. Otherwise, the string is invalid.

## 2. My Approach

1. Create a Stack to store opening brackets.
2. Traverse the string character by character.
3. If the current character is an opening bracket `(`, `[` or `{`, push it into the stack.
4. If it is a closing bracket:
   - Check whether the stack is empty.
   - If empty, return false.
   - Otherwise, pop the top element.
   - Check whether the opening and closing brackets match.
5. If they don't match, return false.
6. After traversing the entire string, check whether the stack is empty.
7. If empty, return true. Otherwise, return false.

## 3. Where I Got Stuck / My Mistake

No major logical mistakes.

The important condition is checking whether the stack is empty before popping.

Also, checking the stack at the end is necessary because there might be unmatched opening brackets.

## 4. What Clicked

The key idea is LIFO (Last In, First Out).

The most recently opened bracket must be the first one to close.

Example:

    s = "{[]}"

    Push '{'
    Push '['
    Encounter ']' → Pop '['
    Encounter '}' → Pop '{'

    Stack = Empty

    Output = true

The Stack naturally handles the nested bracket structure.

## 5. Final Submitted Code

    class Solution {
        public boolean isValid(String s) {
            Stack<Character> st = new Stack<>();

            for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
                if (s.charAt(i) == '(' || s.charAt(i) == '[' || s.charAt(i) == '{') {
                    st.push(s.charAt(i));
                } else {
                    if (st.isEmpty()) return false;

                    char ch = st.pop();

                    if ((ch == '(' && s.charAt(i) == ')') ||
                        (ch == '[' && s.charAt(i) == ']') ||
                        (ch == '{' && s.charAt(i) == '}')) {
                        continue;
                    } else {
                        return false;
                    }
                }
            }

            return st.isEmpty();
        }
    }

## 6. Complexity Analysis

- **Time Complexity:** O(n)
- **Space Complexity:** O(n)

Each character is processed once.

In the worst case, all characters may be opening brackets and stored in the stack.

## 7. Revision Notes

- Stack data structure.
- LIFO (Last In, First Out).
- Push opening brackets.
- Pop when encountering closing brackets.
- Match the correct bracket pairs.
- Check for an empty stack before popping.
- Check whether the stack is empty after traversal.
- O(n) time and O(n) space.

**Key Learning:**

Stack is useful whenever the most recently opened element must be processed first, especially in nested structures.
